   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine>
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_21">2 Avenue S. Philibert</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine>Passy</addrLine>
                            <addrLine>
                                <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                                    <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name>
                                </seg>
                            </addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>To wish you both a happy Xmas and New Year from us both</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1931-12-24">Xmas ’31</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="global-note1" type="global">The face of this postcard reproduces a color illustration of what appear to be violets in a clear bowl-vase of water, with the French Christmas greeting, “Joyeux Noël,” printed beneath. The illustrator is not credited, but the image resembles the work of <name type="person" key="ID_MISSING">Albert Vasset</name>, a draughtsman who lived in Paris in the early 1900s, drew and painted flowers and plants, and was a member of the Société Nationale d’Horticulture de France (see <rs type="letter" key="L_69892">JJ to Pound, 1 January 1931</rs>, notes).</note>
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">After spending months in a series of hotels and flats in <name type="geo" key="G_GBR_LOND">London</name> and other cities, JJ and family returned to <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris</name> and, after some house-hunting, settled into a small, furnished flat at 2 avenue St. Philibert, in Passy, from October 1931 to June 1932.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
   </text>
